--TEST--
All five of a `Core\Queue\Stats`'s counters answer the same scalar, so the dead-letter depth is not a second kind of answer sitting beside the rest — `rule:concurrency/queue-four-members` and `rule:concurrency/attempts-are-finite-and-a-dead-letter-is-kept`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement is the point. § 6 singles the dead-letter depth out as the one
// an unwatched deployment loses work behind, and it would have been easy to
// give it its own shape — a `?uint` that is null until something dies, or a
// state rather than a number. It is a count like the rest, so a program
// watching all five reads them the same way.
var $counts = Core\Queue::stats("flaky");

uint $pending = $counts->pending();
uint $claimed = $counts->claimed();
uint $attempts = $counts->attempts();
uint $dead = $counts->deadLettered();
// And the attempts those buried jobs used are a plain count too, read off the
// dead-letter table the depth above is read off.
uint $buried = $counts->deadAttempts();

// Which is what this line says: the depth is a plain count, so it is not the
// record it came out of and it is not a `Core\Queue\State` either.
Core\Queue\Stats $wrong = $counts->deadLettered();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Queue\Stats`, found `uint`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
